    "source": "1790 census projected to 1792, white adults in Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina and Georgia; only men meeting their state's property or taxpaying rules could vote (Virginia required a freehold). The South Carolina, Georgia and North Carolina legislatures chose their electors, so people there cast no presidential vote."
   },
   {
    "key": "black-south",
    "label": "Black Southerners, most of them enslaved",
    "adults": 0.247,
    "barred": 0.9973,
    "home": 0.0027,
    "A": 0.0,
    "B": 0.0,
    "O": 0.0,
    "source": "1790 census projected to 1792, Black adults in Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina and Georgia; the census gives free people of colour without age or sex before 1820, so adults are estimated. The great majority were enslaved and could not vote. Free Black men meeting the property tests could legally vote only in North Carolina (until its 1835 constitution); the other Southern states limited the vote to white men. In South Carolina, Georgia and North Carolina the legislature chose the electors, so no one there voted for president directly."
   }
